   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 59 of the laws of 2017, relating to penalties arising from late final cost reports, in relation to eligibility for the recovery of such penalties",
    "basePrintNo": "S6404",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6404-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd Part YYY §11, Chap 59 of 2017",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6404\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend chapter 59 of the laws of 2017,\nrelating to penalties arising from late final cost reports, in\nrelation to eligibility for the recovery of such penalties\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo ensure that school districts facing late final cost report\npenalties which exceed five one-hundredths of the school district's\ntotal general fund expenditures are eligible for an optional 10-year\nrecovery period.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision b of section 11 of part YYY of chapter\n59 of the laws of 2017, changing eligibility criteria from six-one\nhundredths to five one-hundredths of a school district's total general\nfund expenditures.\n\nSection 2: Effective Date\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe 2017-18 state budget includes a provision that provides school\ndistricts facing significant penalties due to untimely final cost\nreports filings with an optional 10-year stretch out period to pay the\npenalty. The Newburgh Enlarged City School District's final cost\nreport penalty of $12.7 million more than meets the law's desired\npurpose however, the district was inadvertently left out under the\nadopted budget language. This legislation makes a small technical\nchange to the eligibility criteria to ensure that the Newburgh\nEnlarged City School District eligible for this 10-year stretch out\nperiod provision.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after April 1, 2017.\n",
